Make awk's close() of a pipe return the command's exit status.
close() returned what pclose() returns, the wait status, so a command
that exited with status 3 gave 768, while system() returns the exit
status, as gawk's close() and system() both do. Shift the wait status
as system() does; -1 for a failed pclose() stays -1.

---
 editors/awk.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/awk.c
+++ b/editors/awk.c
@@ -3898,8 +3898,9 @@
 					 * getline line <"doesnt_exist";
 					 * close("doesnt_exist"); <--- here rsm->F is NULL
 					 */
+					/* A pipe gives the exit status, as system() does */
 					if (rsm->F)
-						err = rsm->is_pipe ? pclose(rsm->F) : fclose(rsm->F);
+						err = rsm->is_pipe ? (pclose(rsm->F) >> 8) : fclose(rsm->F);
 					free(rsm->buffer);
 					hash_remove(fdhash, L.s);
 				} else {
